== Chapter I

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The foundation of the whole discourse that follows laid in Rom 8:13 — The words of the apostle explained — The certain connection between true mortification and salvation — Mortification the work of believers — The Spirit the principal efficient cause of it — What is meant by “the body” in the words of the apostle — What by “the deeds of the body” — Life, in what sense promised to this duty.]

#smallcaps[I want] what I have to contribute by way of direction for carrying on the work of mortification in believers to have order and clarity. So I will lay its foundation in these words of the apostle, “if by the Spirit you#super[+] put to death the deeds of the body, you#super[+] will live” (Rom 8:13). And I will make the whole an application of the great gospel truth and mystery contained in them.

The apostle has summed up his doctrine of justification by faith, and the blessed state and condition of those who by grace share in it (verses 1–3 of this chapter). Now he goes on to apply it to the holiness and comfort of believers.

Among his arguments and motives for holiness, the verse I mentioned contains one taken from the opposite outcomes and effects of holiness and of sin: “If you#super[+] live according to the flesh, you#super[+] will die.” What it is to “live according to the flesh,” and what it is to “die,” is not my present aim and business. So I will explain them no further than they fit in with the sense of the later words of the verse, as proposed above.

In the words chosen especially as the foundation of the discourse that follows, there is:

First, A #emph[duty] prescribed: “Put to death the deeds of the body.”

Secondly, The persons to #emph[whom] it is prescribed: “You#super[+],” “if you#super[+] put to death.”

Thirdly, A #emph[promise] attached to that duty: “You#super[+] will live.”

Fourthly, The #emph[cause] or means by which this duty is performed, the Spirit: “If by the Spirit you#super[+].”

Fifthly, The #emph[conditional form] of the whole statement, in which the duty, the means, and the promise are contained: “If you#super[+],” and so on.

1\. The first thing we meet in the words, as they stand in the whole statement, is the conditional word, Εἰ δὲ, “But if.” In statements like this, a condition may mean one of two things:

(1.) The #emph[uncertainty] of the #emph[outcome], the thing promised, as far as those to whom the duty is prescribed are concerned. This is the case where the condition is absolutely necessary to the outcome, and does not itself depend on any fixed cause known to the person to whom it is prescribed. So we say, “If we live, we will do such a thing.” This cannot be what the conditional means in this place. Of the persons to whom these words are spoken, it is said in verse 1 of the same chapter that there is “no condemnation” for them.

(2.) The #emph[certainty] of the #emph[link] and connection between the things spoken of. So we say to a sick man, “If you take this medicine, or use this remedy, you will be well.” All we mean to express is the certainty of the connection between the medicine or remedy and health. And this is its use here. This conditional word points to the certain connection between #emph[putting to death] the deeds of the body and #emph[living].

Now, things are connected in many ways: as cause and effect, or as the way and means to an end. The connection between mortification and life is not one of cause and effect, properly and strictly speaking, for “the gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord” (Rom 6:23). It is a connection of means and end. God has appointed this means for reaching that end, which he has freely promised. Means, though necessary, are rightly subordinate to an end that is freely promised. A gift, and a cause in the receiver that earns it, cannot stand together. So what this statement means, as a conditional, is this: there is a certain, unfailing connection between true mortification and eternal life. If you use this means, you will obtain that end; if you mortify, you will live. And here lies the main motive and force behind the duty prescribed.

2\. The next thing we meet in the words is the #emph[persons] to whom this duty is prescribed. They are expressed in the word “you#super[+],” which in the original is included in the verb θανατοῦτε, “if you#super[+] put to death.” That is, you#super[+] believers; you#super[+] for whom there is “no condemnation” (verse 1); you#super[+] who are “controlled not by the flesh, but by the Spirit” (verse 9); who are given life by the Spirit of Christ (verses 10, 11). To you#super[+] this duty is prescribed. To press this duty directly on anyone else is a clear fruit of the superstition and self-righteousness that the world is full of, the great work and aim of devout men who are ignorant of the gospel (Rom 10:3, 4; John 15:5). Now, this description of the persons, joined with the prescription of the duty, is the main foundation of the discourse that follows, as it stands in this thesis or proposition:

#emph[The choicest believers, who are certainly freed from the condemning power of sin, ought still to make it their business all their days to mortify the indwelling power of sin.]

3\. The principal #emph[efficient cause] of performing this duty is the Spirit: Εἰ δὲ Πνεύματι, “If by the Spirit.” The Spirit here is the Spirit mentioned in verse 11: the Spirit of Christ, the Spirit of God, who “lives in” us (verse 9), who gives us life (verse 11); “the Holy Spirit” (verse 14);#footnote[There seems to be an oversight here, as the expression “Holy Spirit” does not occur in the verse cited. — #smallcaps[Ed].] the “Spirit of sonship” (verse 15); the Spirit who “intercedes for us” (verse 26). All other ways of mortification are vain; all helps leave us helpless. It must be done by the Spirit. Men, as the apostle suggests (Rom 9:30–32), may attempt this work on other principles, by means and advantages offered for other reasons, as they always have done and still do. But, he says, “This is the work of the Spirit. It is to be done by him alone, and by no other power can it be brought about.” Mortification by one’s own strength, carried on by ways of one’s own invention, for the end of one’s own righteousness, is the soul and substance of all false religion in the world. And this is a second principle of the discourse that follows.

4\. The #emph[duty] itself, “Put to death the deeds of the body,” is to be considered next.

Three things are to be asked here: (1.) What is meant by #emph[the body]\; (2.) What by #emph[the deeds of the body]\; (3.) What by #emph[mortifying] them.

(1.) #emph[The body] at the end of the verse is the same as #emph[the flesh] at the beginning: “If you#super[+] live according to the flesh, you#super[+] will die; but if … you#super[+] put to death the deeds of the body,” that is, of the flesh. It is what the apostle has been speaking of all along under the name of #emph[the flesh]. This is clear from the contrast between the Spirit and the flesh that he carries on before and after. #emph[The body], then, here means the corruption and depravity of our natures, of which the body is in large part the seat and instrument; by it the very members of the body are made servants to unrighteousness (Rom 6:19). What is meant is indwelling sin, the corrupted flesh or lust. Many reasons might be given for this figure of speech, which I will not dwell on now. The “body” here is the same as the παλαιὸς ἄνθρωπος and the σῶμα τῆς ἁμαρτίας, the “old man” and the “body of sin” (Rom 6:6). Or it may stand, by naming a part for the whole, for the whole person considered as corrupted, the seat of lusts and disordered affections.

(2.) The #emph[deeds of the body]. The word is πράξεις, which indeed means chiefly the outward actions, “the acts of the flesh,” as they are called, τὰ ἔργα τῆς σαρκός (Gal 5:19), which are there said to be “obvious,” and are listed. Now, though only the outward deeds are named here, the inward and immediate causes are chiefly meant. The “axe is to be laid to the root of the tree”: the deeds of the flesh are to be put to death in the causes from which they spring. The apostle calls them #emph[deeds] because every lust tends toward them. Though it only conceives and miscarries, it aims to bring forth a full-grown sin.

In chapter 7 and at the beginning of this chapter he has dealt with indwelling lust and sin as the fountain and source of all sinful actions. Here he speaks of its destruction under the name of the effects it produces. Πράξεις τοῦ σώματος \[the deeds of the body\] amount to the same as φρόνημα τῆς σαρκός, the “wisdom of the flesh” (Rom 8:6), by the same kind of figure as before. Or they are like the παθήματα and ἐπιθυμίαι, the “passions and lusts of the flesh” (Gal 5:24), from which its deeds and fruits arise. And in this sense #emph[the body] is used in Rom 8:10: “your body is dead because of sin.”

(3.) To #emph[mortify.] The Greek is Εἰ θανατοῦτε, “if you#super[+] put to death.” It is a figure taken from putting any living thing to death. To kill a man, or any living thing, is to take away the source of all its strength, vigor, and power, so that it can no longer act or do anything of its own. So it is here. Indwelling sin is pictured as a person, a living person, called “the old man,” with all its faculties and qualities: its wisdom, craft, cunning, and strength. This, says the apostle, must be killed, put to death, mortified. That is, the Spirit must take away its power, life, vigor, and strength to produce its effects. By the merit of Christ’s cross, and with his death as the pattern of ours, sin has indeed already been utterly put to death. So our “old man” is said to have been “crucified with Him” (Rom 6:6), and we ourselves to have “died with Christ” (verse 8). The work is also truly begun in us at regeneration (Rom 6:3–5), when a principle that opposes sin and destroys it (Gal 5:17) is planted in our hearts. But the whole work must be carried on by degrees toward perfection all our days. More will be said of this as we go on. What the apostle means by this command, then, is this: #emph[it is the constant duty of believers to put to death the indwelling sin that remains in their mortal bodies, so that it has no life or power to bring forth the works or deeds of the flesh.]

5\. The #emph[promise] attached to this duty is life: “You#super[+] will live.” The life promised is set against the death threatened in the clause before, “If you#super[+] live according to the flesh, you#super[+] will die.” The same apostle puts it this way: “from the flesh will reap destruction” (Gal 6:8), that is, destruction from God. Now, perhaps the word means not only eternal life, but also the spiritual life in Christ that we have here. Not its essence and being, which believers already enjoy, but its joy, comfort, and vigor. As the apostle says in another case, “now we can go on living, as long as you#super[+] are standing firm” (1 Thess 3:8); that is, “Now my life will do me good; I will have joy and comfort in my life.” So, “You#super[+] will live: you#super[+] will lead a good, vigorous, spiritual life, full of comfort, while you#super[+] are here, and obtain eternal life hereafter.”

Taking for granted what was said before about the connection between mortification and eternal life, as of means and end, I will add only this, as a second motive to the duty prescribed:

#emph[The vigor, and power, and comfort of our spiritual life depend on the mortification of the deeds of the flesh.]
